top -- Command

Display the processes using the most processor time
top [-ISbinqu] [-d count] [-s seconds] [-U user] [number]

top shows a summary of the machine and then the processes using the most
processor time, redrawing the display every few seconds until you quit it.  It
reads the process table and the segment tables out of the running kernel
through /dev/kmem, and each process's u-area through /dev/mem or /dev/swap, so
it is installed setuid to root.

The summary is four lines: the number of processes and how many are in each
state; the percentage of processor time spent in user, system and idle over
the last interval; the load average over one, five and fifteen minutes; and
memory, taken from the kernel's own segment arena.  Below them is one line per
process, ordered by processor use.

    PID		the process id
    USERNAME	who owns it (-u shows the numeric uid instead)
    NICE	the nice value, as the kernel holds it
    SIZE	the total size of the process's segments
    RES		the resident part of that
    STATE	sleep, wait, run, stop or zomb
    TIME	processor time used, minutes and seconds
    %CPU	share of the processor over the last interval
    COMMAND	the command name

Options
    -b		Batch mode: write plain text and do not read the keyboard.
		This is the default when the output is not a terminal.
    -d count	Show count displays and exit.
    -i		Interactive: read the keyboard even if the output is not a
		terminal.
    -I		Show idle processes as well.
    -n		The same as -b.
    -q		Run at the highest priority.  Root only; a warning
		otherwise.
    -s seconds	Wait seconds between displays.  The default is 5.
    -S		Show system processes as well.
    -u		Print numeric user ids instead of names.
    -U user	Show only that user's processes.
    number	Show at most that many processes.

The environment variable TOP is read before the command line and may hold any
of these, so a preferred set of options need not be typed each time.

Interactive Commands
While a display is up, a single character does the following.

    ^L	Redraw the screen.
    d	Change the number of displays still to show.
    e	List the errors from the last kill or renice.
    h, ?	Show the interactive commands.
    i, I	Toggle the display of idle processes.
    k	Send a signal to a list of processes.
    n, #	Change the number of processes shown.
    q	Quit.
    r	Renice a process.
    s	Change the seconds between displays.
    u	Show one user's processes; `+' brings back all of them.

The terminal is driven through the termcap description of $TERM.  Once the
first display is drawn, only the characters that changed are rewritten, so an
update costs a few dozen characters rather than a screenful -- which matters
on a serial console.  A terminal that cannot address the cursor gets the batch
display, and the commands that need cursor addressing are withheld with a note
saying so.

Diagnostics
    renice: this kernel has no setpriority(2)

The `r' command cannot work here.  This kernel's nice(2) is the V7 one, which
a process may only apply to itself, so nothing can renice another process and
the command says so rather than appearing to succeed.

The load average is read from the kernel's own counter.  A kernel too old to
keep one has no symbol for it, and the three figures are then shown as dashes
rather than as whatever happened to be at that address.

Files
/dev/kmem	the process and segment tables
/dev/mem	u-areas of resident processes
/dev/swap	u-areas of processes that are not resident
/coherent	the kernel image the symbol table is read from

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article behind it, and no COHERENT holding
carries a top: the program is William LeFebvre's, release 3.3, ported here.
Its own manual page is not among this project's holdings, so this page was
written here from the source that is; none of Mark Williams Company's text and
none of the upstream page's text is in it.  The copyright notices and terms
that came with the program are kept beside its source, unaltered.

WHAT IS OURS is the part that knows this kernel: the walk of the process
queue, the segment arithmetic behind SIZE, RES and the memory line, the
u-area read, and the processor-state figures, which are computed from the
per-process time deltas between one display and the next.

Nothing in top computes in floating point.  Every quantity it prints through
what looks like a floating conversion -- the load average, the processor
percentages, the times -- is an integer in fixed point and is printed as one,
which is a representation the program supports and not a local fork.

There is no mpid in this kernel, so the summary does not report the last
process id started.

